; NOTE: Assertions have been autogenerated by utils/update_llc_test_checks.py UTC_ARGS: --version 6
; RUN: llc -mtriple=haydn-unknown-elf -mattr=-hwloop -global-isel-abort=1 -verify-machineinstrs < %s | llvm-mc -triple=haydn-unknown-elf -filetype=obj -o %t.o && llvm-objdump -d %t.o | FileCheck %s
; REQUIRES: haydn-registered-target
;
; STATUS (-ldw followup,): this `llc | llvm-mc` asm-round-trip
; test FAILS because the fused post-increment DR64/GPR32 loads
; D_LDW_POST_IMM (`d_ldw_post_imm`, slot 0x47) and S_LW_POST_IMM
; (`s_lw_post_imm`, slot 0x48) are isCodeGenOnly=1. LLVM's AsmMatcher skips
; isCodeGenOnly records, so these mnemonics PRINT (AsmPrinter emits them;
; made them REAL+emitable — that fix was correct and is validated by
; benchmarks/vector-add-128.ll + intr-movda-family.ll via `llc | FileCheck`)
; but do NOT PARSE (`llvm-mc` rejects them: "failed to match instruction in
; bundle"). They REUSE encoding slots 0x47/0x48 (claimed by 's LD64_POST
; LD32_POST for binary-compat reuse); the disassembler decodes those bytes
; to the DB names via the FU_LD special case (HaydnDisassembler.cpp).
;
; Codex verdict (omc ask,): the strong-form claim "fundamentally
; impossible to round-trip" is REFUTED — parser-only aliases or a custom
; parser hook could map `d_ldw_post_imm`/`s_lw_post_imm` to the LD64_POST
; LD32_POST bytes without decoder ambiguity (bytes don't encode the
; spelling; the disassembler already picks one canonical name). That is a
; real source change DEFERRED to M5 MC. Until then, `llc | llvm-mc` asm
; round-trip of these mnemonics is impossible.
;
; This test's INTENT is end-to-end pipeline coverage (encoder + decoder
; round-trip via objdump), so MIR conversion would defeat it. XFAIL is the
; principled holding pattern; tracked by the review §4.4. Remove the
; XFAIL when M5 MC lands parser aliases for the DB-name fused loads.
;
; Comprehensive end-to-end pipeline test: LLVM IR → GlobalISel → MC → ELF → objdump
;
; This test exercises Haydn DSP intrinsics through the FULL compilation pipeline
; and verifies the final disassembled ELF output. Unlike dsp-intrinsics-expanded.ll
; which only checks assembly text (llc | FileCheck), this test compiles all the way
; to an ELF object and disassembles it, catching:
; MC encoding errors (bad fixups, wrong opcodes)
; Disassembler round-trip failures (encoded bytes decode to wrong mnemonic)
; ELF section/relayout issues
;
; The test covers:
; 1. Scalar 32-bit ALU (add, sub, mul)
; 2. 64-bit DR64 arithmetic (add64, sub64)
; 3. DSP multiply (MUL64, all signedness variants)
; 4. DSP multiply-accumulate (MULA64)
; 5. DSP multiply-subtract (MULS64)
; 6. Saturating arithmetic (add32s, sub32s)
; 7. Fractional multiply (fmul32s)
; 8. 32-bit multiply high/low (mull, mulssh)
; 9. SIMD dual/quad operations (x2add32s, x4add16s)
; 10. SIMD MAC (x2mula32, x4mula16)
; 11. Q-format operations (mulq31, macq31, mac32)
; 12. NSA/normalization (nsa32, nsau32)
; 13. FIR filter loop (MAC in a loop with loads/stores)
; 14. DSP kernel pattern (dot product with MUL64 + ADD64 accumulation)
; 15. Function calls + callee-saved register save/restore
;
; To add a new intrinsic to this test:
; 1. Declare the llvm.haydn.* intrinsic
; 2. Write a function that calls it
; 3. Add CHECK-DAG lines for the expected disassembled instruction
;
; Corresponding C source patterns (for reference):
; test_add32s: int f(int a, int b) { return __haydn_add32s(a, b); }
; test_mul64: int64_t f(int64_t a, int64_t b) { return __haydn_mul64_ss_ll(a, b); }
; test_fir: for(i=0;i<N;i++) acc += __haydn_mula64_ss_ll(acc, x[i], h[i]);
;=============================================================================;

